{"st":"AL","name":"Alabama","seats":[{"census":1810,"seats":1},{"census":1820,"seats":3},{"census":1830,"seats":5},{"census":1840,"seats":7},{"census":1850,"seats":7},{"census":1860,"seats":6},{"census":1870,"seats":8},{"census":1880,"seats":8},{"census":1890,"seats":9},{"census":1900,"seats":9},{"census":1910,"seats":10},{"census":1920,"seats":10},{"census":1930,"seats":9},{"census":1940,"seats":9},{"census":1950,"seats":9},{"census":1960,"seats":8},{"census":1970,"seats":7},{"census":1980,"seats":7},{"census":1990,"seats":7},{"census":2000,"seats":7},{"census":2010,"seats":7},{"census":2020,"seats":7}],"events":[{"congress":16,"year":1819,"kind":"statehood","title":"Statehood","text":"Alabama became the 22nd state on December 14, 1819. Congress gave it 1 seat in the House until the next apportionment.","sources":["https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/R47747.html","https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":18,"year":1822,"kind":"census","title":"1820 census apportionment","text":"After the 1820 census, Alabama's House delegation grew from 1 to 3 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":23,"year":1832,"kind":"census","title":"1830 census apportionment","text":"After the 1830 census, Alabama's House delegation grew from 3 to 5 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":27,"year":1840,"kind":"at-large","title":"Statewide (general ticket) elections","text":"In the 27th Congress (1841–1843), Alabama elected all of its representatives statewide on a general ticket instead of by district.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://history.house.gov/Institution/Apportionment/Apportionment/"]},{"congress":28,"year":1842,"kind":"census","title":"1840 census apportionment","text":"After the 1840 census, Alabama's House delegation grew from 5 to 7 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":33,"year":1852,"kind":"census","title":"1850 census apportionment","text":"After the 1850 census, Alabama kept 7 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":38,"year":1862,"kind":"census","title":"1860 census apportionment","text":"After the 1860 census, Alabama's House delegation shrank from 7 to 6 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":43,"year":1872,"kind":"census","title":"1870 census apportionment","text":"After the 1870 census, Alabama's House delegation grew from 6 to 8 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":43,"year":1872,"kind":"at-large","title":"Some members elected at-large","text":"In the 43rd through 44th Congresses (1873–1877), Alabama elected 2 of its representatives statewide (at-large) and the rest by district.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://history.house.gov/Institution/Apportionment/Apportionment/"]},{"congress":48,"year":1882,"kind":"census","title":"1880 census apportionment","text":"After the 1880 census, Alabama kept 8 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":53,"year":1892,"kind":"census","title":"1890 census apportionment","text":"After the 1890 census, Alabama's House delegation grew from 8 to 9 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":58,"year":1902,"kind":"census","title":"1900 census apportionment","text":"After the 1900 census, Alabama kept 9 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":63,"year":1912,"kind":"census","title":"1910 census apportionment","text":"After the 1910 census, Alabama's House delegation grew from 9 to 10 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":63,"year":1912,"kind":"at-large","title":"Some members elected at-large","text":"In the 63rd through 64th Congresses (1913–1917), Alabama elected one of its representatives statewide (at-large) and the rest by district.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://history.house.gov/Institution/Apportionment/Apportionment/"]},{"congress":73,"year":1932,"kind":"census","title":"1930 census apportionment","text":"After the 1930 census, Alabama's House delegation shrank from 10 to 9 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":78,"year":1942,"kind":"census","title":"1940 census apportionment","text":"After the 1940 census, Alabama kept 9 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":83,"year":1952,"kind":"census","title":"1950 census apportionment","text":"After the 1950 census, Alabama kept 9 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":88,"year":1962,"kind":"census","title":"1960 census apportionment","text":"After the 1960 census, Alabama's House delegation shrank from 9 to 8 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":88,"year":1962,"kind":"at-large","title":"Statewide (general ticket) elections","text":"In the 88th Congress (1963–1965), Alabama elected all of its representatives statewide on a general ticket instead of by district.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://history.house.gov/Institution/Apportionment/Apportionment/"]},{"congress":88,"year":1964,"kind":"court","title":"Reynolds v. Sims","text":"In this Alabama case the Supreme Court held that state legislative districts must also have roughly equal populations. It concerned the state legislature, not Congress.","sources":["https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/377/533"]},{"congress":93,"year":1972,"kind":"census","title":"1970 census apportionment","text":"After the 1970 census, Alabama's House delegation shrank from 8 to 7 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":98,"year":1982,"kind":"census","title":"1980 census apportionment","text":"After the 1980 census, Alabama kept 7 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":103,"year":1992,"kind":"census","title":"1990 census apportionment","text":"After the 1990 census, Alabama kept 7 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":108,"year":2002,"kind":"census","title":"2000 census apportionment","text":"After the 2000 census, Alabama kept 7 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":113,"year":2012,"kind":"census","title":"2010 census apportionment","text":"After the 2010 census, Alabama kept 7 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":118,"year":2022,"kind":"census","title":"2020 census apportionment","text":"After the 2020 census, Alabama kept 7 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":119,"year":2023,"kind":"court","title":"Allen v. Milligan","text":"The Supreme Court upheld a lower court finding that Alabama's 2021 congressional map likely violated Section 2 of the Voting Rights Act. A court-drawn map with a second district where Black voters could elect their preferred candidate was used from the 2024 election.","sources":["https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/21-1086","https://redistricting.lls.edu/state/alabama/"]},{"congress":119,"year":2025,"kind":"court","title":"Finding of intentional discrimination","text":"In May 2025 the federal court that drew the 2024 map found that the legislature's 2023 plan intentionally discriminated against Black voters, and it kept its own map in place.","sources":["https://redistricting.lls.edu/state/alabama/","https://www.kpbs.org/news/national/2025/05/09/court-rules-alabama-redistricting-intentionally-discriminates-against-black-voters"]},{"congress":119,"year":2026,"kind":"court","title":"Case sent back after Louisiana v. Callais","text":"On May 11, 2026 the Supreme Court vacated the order blocking the 2023 plan and sent the case back for review in light of Louisiana v. Callais. On May 26 the federal court again found intentional discrimination and ordered its own map used.","sources":["https://www.mississippifreepress.org/supreme-court-allows-alabama-to-use-congressional-map-favoring-republicans-in-this-years-elections/","https://www.npr.org/2026/05/12/nx-s1-5819600/alabama-congressional-redistricting-special-election"]},{"congress":120,"year":2026,"kind":"court","title":"Supreme Court allows the 2023 map","text":"On June 2, 2026 the Supreme Court blocked that order, so the legislature's 2023 map was used in the 2026 election. Special primaries for the 1st, 2nd, 6th and 7th Districts were held on August 11, 2026.","sources":["https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25","https://www.mississippifreepress.org/supreme-court-allows-alabama-to-use-congressional-map-favoring-republicans-in-this-years-elections/","https://www.local3news.com/local-news/gov-kay-ivey-announces-special-primary-election-after-supreme-court-ruling-on-al-congressional-map/article_c835d356-7f81-4b3d-9a90-3d4a3cf21954.html"]}],"compactness":{"c016":{"AL-0":0.456},"c017":{"AL-0":0.456},"c018":{"AL-1":0.306,"AL-2":0.299,"AL-3":0.28},"c019":{"AL-1":0.306,"AL-2":0.299,"AL-3":0.28},"c020":{"AL-1":0.306,"AL-2":0.299,"AL-3":0.28},"c021":{"AL-1":0.306,"AL-2":0.299,"AL-3":0.28},"c022":{"AL-1":0.306,"AL-2":0.299,"AL-3":0.28},"c023":{"AL-1":0.431,"AL-2":0.514,"AL-3":0.303,"AL-4":0.548,"AL-5":0.207},"c024":{"AL-1":0.431,"AL-2":0.514,"AL-3":0.303,"AL-4":0.548,"AL-5":0.207},"c025":{"AL-1":0.431,"AL-2":0.514,"AL-3":0.303,"AL-4":0.548,"AL-5":0.207},"c026":{"AL-1":0.431,"AL-2":0.514,"AL-3":0.303,"AL-4":0.548,"AL-5":0.207},"c027":{"AL-0":0.456},"c028":{"AL-1":0.208,"AL-2":0.463,"AL-3":0.364,"AL-4":0.327,"AL-5":0.525,"AL-6":0.426,"AL-7":0.37},"c029":{"AL-1":0.208,"AL-2":0.463,"AL-3":0.364,"AL-4":0.327,"AL-5":0.525,"AL-6":0.426,"AL-7":0.37},"c030":{"AL-1":0.208,"AL-2":0.463,"AL-3":0.364,"AL-4":0.327,"AL-5":0.525,"AL-6":0.426,"AL-7":0.37},"c031":{"AL-1":0.208,"AL-2":0.463,"AL-3":0