as not been researched yet.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":2},"c093":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1970 census, which cut Alabama's seats from 8 to 7. It was first used in the 1972 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":8},"c098":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1980 census, which left Alabama with 7 seats. It was first used in the 1982 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":4},"c103":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1990 census, which left Alabama with 7 seats. It was first used in the 1992 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":7},"c108":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 2000 census, which left Alabama with 7 seats. It was first used in the 2002 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":7},"c113":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 2010 census, which left Alabama with 7 seats. It was first used in the 2012 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":7},"c115":{"why":"small","kind":"other","text":"Not a new map: the historical boundary records show only slight differences in one district from the previous Congress, and no statewide redistricting is recorded for this election.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":1},"c118":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 2020 census, which left Alabama with 7 seats. It was first used in the 2022 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":7},"c119":{"why":"event","kind":"court","text":"New map following: Allen v. Milligan. The Supreme Court upheld a lower court finding that Alabama's 2021 congressional map likely violated Section 2 of the Voting Rights Act. A court-drawn map with a second district where Black voters could elect their preferred candidate was used from the 2024 election.","sources":["https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/21-1086","https://redistricting.lls.edu/state/alabama/"],"districtsChanged":7},"c120":{"why":"event","kind":"court","text":"New map following: Supreme Court allows the 2023 map. On June 2, 2026 the Supreme Court blocked that order, so the legislature's 2023 map was used in the 2026 election. Special primaries for the 1st, 2nd, 6th and 7th Districts were held on August 11, 2026.","sources":["https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25","https://www.mississippifreepress.org/supreme-court-allows-alabama-to-use-congressional-map-favoring-republicans-in-this-years-elections/","https://www.local3news.com/local-news/gov-kay-ivey-announces-special-primary-election-after-supreme-court-ruling-on-al-congressional-map/article_c835d356-7f81-4b3d-9a90-3d4a3cf21954.html"],"districtsChanged":4}}}